rutabaga_gfx: native contexts through the FFI, the fence ring index, polling, a logger, guest dma-bufs, cross-domain hang-ups

The research's patches/rutabaga_gfx/changes.diff (git diff HEAD of its
checkout of 4fb9f32055d8, release 0.1.85, on 2026-10-12; the cross-domain
hang-ups added on 2026-10-15, its commits 8ecffaf and b4400b1), unchanged
below this description:

- the FFI's virgl_renderer feature: DRM native contexts and virgl2
  through the C FFI that QEMU uses, beside cross-domain on one device,
  as crosvm's --features virgl_renderer;
- struct rutabaga_fence's ring_idx a uint8_t, as RutabagaFence's is in
  Rust: as a uint32_t a reader got three bytes of padding with it, the
  fences of rings other than 0 never matched their commands, and QEMU's
  control queue filled up (the display froze);
- rutabaga_poll_descriptor() and rutabaga_poll(): virglrenderer's DRM
  contexts retire their fences only from a poll;
- rutabaga's and virglrenderer's messages to stderr, which QEMU dropped
  (no logger): warnings and errors, RUTABAGA_LOG=off|error|warn|info|
  debug|trace for others, at most 50 at once then one a second;
- guest-memory blobs get the udmabuf of their pages QEMU makes
  (x-guest-dmabuf), through virglrenderer's
  virgl_renderer_resource_set_guest_dmabuf(), looked up at run time;
- cross-domain hang-ups: when the host end of the guest's Wayland
  connection goes away (the app serving QEMU's socket quit or crashed),
  the guest gets one empty receive, which says so, and the channel is no
  longer polled: every guest poll got another one at once, QEMU spun at
  about 3 CPUs and the guest's session ran on without a window.  An app
  that dies with data it has not read resets the connection instead of
  ending it: a reset, abort, broken pipe or not-connected error on the
  receive is that end too, where it halted the worker and the guest never
  heard of it.  A failed connect at the context's start (nothing serves
  the socket: ENOENT, ECONNREFUSED) gives the guest a channel already at
  its end instead of none, whose polls were never answered: the session
  hung.

